I checked 6 Larch Grove. It is not in a conservation area, is not a listed building, and has no tree preservation order. That helps, since those are the cases where the rules are stricter.

The council guidance on outbuildings says garden rooms and sheds usually don't need an application if they meet all three of these conditions:

1. The building is under 2.5 metres high.
2. It sits behind the front wall of the house.
3. It covers less than half the garden.

Based on the constraints check, 6 Larch Grove has no restrictions that would change this. Whether your plans meet the three conditions depends on the actual dimensions, height and position, which I can't check from here. If your garden room is 2.5 metres or taller, sits in front of the house, or would cover half the garden or more, you will likely need an application.

I can't give a formal planning opinion. If you're unsure, or if the design goes beyond these conditions, you can submit an application or ask the council for a formal determination. You're also welcome to send me the measurements, and I'll tell you which conditions they affect.
